Article 4
Every Woman has the right: a to life and the right to maintain her life and livelihood;
b to form a family and continue descendants through legal marriage;
c to develop herself through the fulfillment of her basic needs; d to obtain education and benefit from science and technology, arts and culture, in order to improve the quality of her life and for the welfare of humankind; e to advance herself in fighting for her rights collectively to build society, nation and state; f to recognition, guarantee, protection and fair legal certainty as well as equal treatment before the law;
g to work and receive fair and proper remuneration and treatment in employment relations;
h to obtain equal opportunities in government; i to freedom of association, assembly and expression of opinion; j to protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property under her control, and the right to a sense of security and protection from threats of fear to do or not do something which is a basic right;
k to be free from torture or treatment that degrades human dignity and the right to obtain political asylum from other countries;
l to live in physical and spiritual prosperity, to reside and obtain a good and healthy living environment, and the right to obtain health services;
m to social security that enables her full development as a dignified human being;
n to equality of position in law and government;
o to work and a livelihood worthy of humanity; p in efforts to defend the state;
q to communicate and obtain information to develop her personal and social environment, and the right to seek, obtain, possess, store, process, and convey information using all types of available channels;
r to embrace her respective religion and to worship according to her religion and belief; and s to obtain education.

Article 26
The Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in Article 25 shall be in the form of: a counseling to stakeholders regarding children's rights;
b assistance with psychological, medical and legal services and socio-economic reintegration;
c family empowerment through provision of training, business capital stimulus and mentoring;
d scholarships to continue formal education to higher levels; and e non-formal education and skills training for those who do not pursue formal education.

